diligent-engine provides CMake targets:

    find_package(unofficial-diligent-engine CONFIG REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE
        unofficial::diligent-engine::DiligentCore
        unofficial::diligent-engine::DiligentTools   # with the "tools" feature
        unofficial::diligent-engine::DiligentFX      # with the "fx" feature
    )

Headers keep their upstream layout, so both include styles work:

    #include "Graphics/GraphicsEngine/interface/RenderDevice.h"
    #include "DiligentCore/Graphics/GraphicsEngine/interface/RenderDevice.h"

The samples and tutorials are not built by this port. The Metal backend is not
available: it ships only in the closed-source DiligentCorePro module.

On a dynamic triplet the graphics backends are additionally installed as shared
libraries, each with its own target:

    unofficial::diligent-engine::GraphicsEngineVk
    unofficial::diligent-engine::GraphicsEngineOpenGL
    unofficial::diligent-engine::GraphicsEngineD3D11    # Windows
    unofficial::diligent-engine::GraphicsEngineD3D12    # Windows
    unofficial::diligent-engine::Archiver

Link one of those to get the backend as a shared library; link only
DiligentCore to get it statically. Either way the factory is reached by calling
GetEngineFactoryVk()/GetEngineFactoryOpenGL()/... directly.

DiligentCore, DiligentTools and DiligentFX are always static archives -- upstream
builds no shared flavour of them, so a dynamic triplet gets those plus the shared
backends, matching what a system-wide install of Diligent produces.

To load a backend at runtime instead of linking it (Windows/MSVC only), define
ENGINE_DLL=1 in your own target and call LoadGraphicsEngineVk().

With the "vulkan" feature there is one more target:

    unofficial::diligent-engine::volk

Nothing has to link it to run: the engine dispatches every Vulkan call through
volk and carries its own copy. It is for a process that replaces volkInitialize
-- the way to point the engine at a loader the stock one cannot find, since a
leaf name and /usr/local/lib are all it tries. That means compiling volk.c,
which the volk port installs beside volk.h:

    #define volkInitialize volkInitializeStock
    #include <volk.c>
    #undef volkInitialize
    VkResult volkInitialize(void) { /* dlopen, then volkInitializeCustom */ }

The target names volk's headers and the WSI definitions the engine's own volk
was built with, which that translation unit has to compile with. Compiled with
less, it is missing the surface entry point the engine calls, the engine's copy
is pulled in beside it, and every loader symbol is defined twice.
